WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.800
Raspberry AI is a generative fashion design platform,
and this is Boards, their new canvas workspace.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.340
I designed a new masking mode for Boards
for their Sketch to Render feature,

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.200
to apply different materials
to specific parts of a garment.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.760
Previously, masking
was done in this modal,

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.980
but for Boards, I decided to move the tools onto a
toolbar to keep the rest of the workspace available.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.860
Let me show you how it works.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.000
First, the designer enters masking mode
through the Sketch to Render node,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.130
then selects Add Mask
to draw on their mask.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.020
Once complete, they can add
more materials, up to three.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.380
Here, they select a second one.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.440
Click on the second material to enter masking
mode again to add that second mask.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.860
To see the different masks they’ve applied, they can
click on the material in the toolbar or in the node.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.000
And once all materials have been masked
and a prompt added,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.400
Generate is enabled
and the designs are rendered.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.760
So the main reason I chose masking mode
with a toolbar over a modal was

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.000
so that designers could reference other
ideas on the canvas while they work.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.380
As you can see, the toolbar here stays with the image
so they don’t lose track of what’s being edited.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.500
And if they were to pan away,
a snackbar appears,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.080
allowing them to quickly return
to the image or exit masking mode.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.480
Zooming in or out also maintains the same
toolbar size so that it always stays usable.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.280
The result is a new masking interaction that keeps
designers in context on the canvas without breaking their workflow.
